Rivet supports named C++ modules and conventional headers. A module exposes declarations with `export`; consumers use `import` and link the module's object code. See [Clang's module guide](https://clang.llvm.org/docs/StandardCPlusPlusModules.html#quick-start) for the language model.

## Creation options

| Option | Values and behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `--modules` | `true` by default in C++20/23/26; `false` in C++17. Controls project interfaces. |
| `--std-modules` | `false` by default. Enables `import std;`; requires C++23+ and a compatible toolchain. |
| `--module-layout` | `split` (default) or `single`. Controls the initial named-module layout. |
| `--header-guard` | `pragma` (default) or `ifndef`. Applies to headers, not module units. |

The project-interface and standard-library choices are independent:

| Project interfaces | Standard library | CLI flags |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Named modules | Headers | `--modules true --std-modules false` |
| Named modules | Modules | `--modules true --std-modules true` |
| Headers | Modules | `--modules false --std-modules true` |
| Headers | Headers | `--modules false --std-modules false` |

Rivet requires Ninja when either module option is enabled. It offers Unix Makefiles when both are disabled. Changing manifest options alone does not convert existing source or build files.

## Generated files and templates

| Choice | Generated structure |
| --- | --- |
| Single-file module | `core.cppm` contains declarations and definitions. |
| Interface + implementation | `core.cppm` declares the interface; `core.cpp` defines its functions. |
| `module` file template | One `.cppm` interface; definitions may live there. |
| `module_pair` file template | Matching interface and implementation files. |
| `module_impl` file template | Implementation unit for an existing module; CLI requires `--module NAME`. |

Interfaces belong to a `CXX_MODULES` file set; implementation units belong to the ordinary source list. Consumers link the owning library. Module names, filenames, and CMake target names are independent. `.cppm` is Rivet's file convention. See [CMake's module guide](https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/manual/cmake-cxxmodules.7.html).

## Single-file and split syntax

A single-file interface, `memory.cppm`:

```cpp
export module engine.memory;

export namespace engine {
int page_size() { return 4096; }
}
```

A consumer, `main.cpp`:

```cpp
import engine.memory;

int main() {
  return engine::page_size() == 4096 ? 0 : 1;
}
```

For a split module, replace the definition in `memory.cppm` with `int page_size();` and put the body in `memory.cpp`:

```cpp
module engine.memory;

namespace engine {
int page_size() { return 4096; }
}
```

The implementation unit implicitly imports its primary interface. Keep each non-inline function definition in one unit. See the [C++ module-unit rules](https://eel.is/c++draft/module.unit).

## Include and import placement

Put conventional headers in the global module fragment, before the named module declaration:

```cpp
module;
#include <cstddef>

export module engine.memory;

export std::size_t page_size();
```

With standard-library modules enabled:

```cpp
export module engine.memory;
import std;

export std::size_t page_size();
```

In a module unit, imports follow the module declaration and precede other declarations in its purview. See the [import rules](https://eel.is/c++draft/module.import) and [global module fragment rules](https://eel.is/c++draft/module.global.frag).

Named-module imports do not expose macros. Header-unit imports such as `import <vector>;` can expose them, but Rivet does not generate header units and CMake's documented module support excludes them.

## Standard-library module requirements

Rivet's generated helper accepts **CMake 4.4.0–4.4.4** and enables the [release-specific experimental gate](https://github.com/Kitware/CMake/blob/v4.4.4/Help/dev/experimental.rst) before compiler discovery. Configuration checks the selected compiler and library for C++23 or C++26 `import std` support. The [libc++ module documentation](https://libcxx.llvm.org/Modules.html) describes its compiler and library requirements; [Compatibility](/docs/compatibility/) records Rivet's tested combinations.

The helper can locate libc++ module metadata beside the selected compiler. An explicit `CMAKE_CXX_STDLIB_MODULES_JSON` in `CMakeUserPresets.json` takes precedence. Unsupported combinations produce an error.

```sh
rivet new ./compute --name compute \
  --modules true --std-modules true --build
```

The first build generates module artifacts and a compilation database. Clangd's [module support is experimental](https://clangd.llvm.org/features#experimental-c20-modules-support); editor diagnostics can differ from compiler results. See [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting/) after confirming the build succeeds.
